WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.688
<v Examiner>The words of genetics.

2
00:00:02.688 --> 00:00:05.826
<v Examiner>Her student-marked quiz has you match sixteen of them.

3
00:00:06.276 --> 00:00:12.283
<v Examiner>Define these terms: homozygous, heterozygous, allele, dominant, recessive, genotype,

4
00:00:12.283 --> 00:00:18.361
<v Examiner>phenotype, autosomal, X-linked, locus, chromosomes, genome, autosome, sex chromosome,

5
00:00:18.361 --> 00:00:20.292
<v Examiner>variation, and inheritance.

6
00:00:20.742 --> 00:00:24.037
Two of her checklist points are nothing but definitions,

7
00:00:24.037 --> 00:00:28.038
and she re-asks the same dozen words across eight different quizzes.

8
00:00:28.038 --> 00:00:30.274
She never wants an essay about a term.

9
00:00:30.274 --> 00:00:33.098
She gives you the definition and wants the word,

10
00:00:33.098 --> 00:00:35.863
or gives you the word and wants the definition.

11
00:00:35.863 --> 00:00:38.570
So these are the cheapest marks in the module.

12
00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.923
Every definition here is her quiz key's.

13
00:00:41.373 --> 00:00:43.999
Start with the pair everyone mixes up.

14
00:00:43.999 --> 00:00:47.660
Homozygous: the alleles of the genotype are the same.

15
00:00:47.660 --> 00:00:50.009
Big B big B, or little b little b.

16
00:00:50.009 --> 00:00:51.114
Homo means same.

17
00:00:51.114 --> 00:00:54.983
Heterozygous: the alleles of the genotype are different.

18
00:00:54.983 --> 00:00:56.019
Big B little b.

19
00:00:56.019 --> 00:00:57.608
Hetero means different.

20
00:00:58.058 --> 00:01:00.955
An allele is a different version of a gene.

21
00:01:00.955 --> 00:01:04.729
Her example: a diseased gene versus a non-diseased gene.

22
00:01:04.729 --> 00:01:09.109
Her other wording: alternative forms of genes are called alleles.

23
00:01:09.559 --> 00:01:13.306
Dominant: it will always appear in the phenotype, if it's there.

24
00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:18.518
It suppresses the expression of the other allele, and it's written with a capital letter.

25
00:01:18.518 --> 00:01:23.203
Recessive: it will only appear in the phenotype if no other alleles are present,

26
00:01:23.203 --> 00:01:25.194
so only when there are two copies.

27
00:01:25.194 --> 00:01:26.775
It's written in lower case.

28
00:01:28.225 --> 00:01:32.141
Genotype: the alleles that have been inherited by an individual.

29
00:01:32.141 --> 00:01:32.875
The letters.

30
00:01:32.875 --> 00:01:37.403
Phenotype: how the traits are expressed and physically seen in the person,

31
00:01:37.403 --> 00:01:38.994
like freckles you can see.

32
00:01:38.994 --> 00:01:43.094
Her other wording: the expression of genes is called the phenotype.

33
00:01:43.544 --> 00:01:48.216
Autosomal: when a disease gene is found on chromosomes one to twenty-two.

34
00:01:48.216 --> 00:01:51.992
X-linked: when a disease gene is found on the X chromosome,

35
00:01:51.992 --> 00:01:55.448
and it generally shows up in greater numbers in males.

36
00:01:55.898 --> 00:01:58.894
Locus: where a gene is located on a chromosome.

37
00:01:58.894 --> 00:02:01.061
Think of it as the gene's address.

38
00:02:01.511 --> 00:02:06.265
Chromosomes: a length of DNA that has a collection of genes on it.

39
00:02:06.265 --> 00:02:07.922
They're found in pairs.

40
00:02:07.922 --> 00:02:11.164
Genome: all the DNA found within the nucleus.

41
00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:14.766
Autosome: a chromosome numbered one to twenty-two.

42
00:02:14.766 --> 00:02:16.999
Sex chromosome: the X or the Y.

43
00:02:17.449 --> 00:02:21.149
Variation: differences in the genes found in populations.

44
00:02:21.149 --> 00:02:24.979
Inheritance: how genes are passed from parents to children.

45
00:02:26.429 --> 00:02:28.997
Five more words her other quizzes use.

46
00:02:28.997 --> 00:02:33.119
A gene: a segment of DNA that codes for a trait or a protein.

47
00:02:33.119 --> 00:02:39.133
A carrier: a heterozygous person who can pass on a recessive condition without having it.

48
00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:42.985
Heredity: the passing of traits from parents to children.

49
00:02:42.985 --> 00:02:48.661
Genetics: the study of how traits are inherited through the interactions of alleles.

50
00:02:48.661 --> 00:02:53.054
And a phenocopy: a phenotype that looks like a genetic condition,

51
00:02:53.054 --> 00:02:56.838
but is caused by the environment, with no genetic basis.

52
00:02:57.288 --> 00:02:57.991
The traps.

53
00:02:57.991 --> 00:03:03.895
Autosomal describes where a disease gene sits; an autosome is the chromosome itself.

54
00:03:03.895 --> 00:03:07.972
The genome is all of the DNA; a gene is one segment of it.

55
00:03:07.972 --> 00:03:11.837
And a chromosome isn't a gene: it carries many of them.

56
00:03:12.287 --> 00:03:12.945
<v Examiner>Once more.

57
00:03:12.945 --> 00:03:13.866
<v Examiner>Sixteen words.

58
00:03:16.316 --> 00:03:19.043
Homozygous: the alleles are the same.

59
00:03:19.043 --> 00:03:21.401
Heterozygous: they're different.

60
00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:23.465
Allele: a version of a gene.

61
00:03:23.465 --> 00:03:26.560
Dominant: always appears in the phenotype.

62
00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.540
Recessive: appears only if no other allele is present.

63
00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:32.898
Genotype: the alleles inherited.

64
00:03:32.898 --> 00:03:35.404
Phenotype: the traits you can see.

65
00:03:35.404 --> 00:03:39.752
Autosomal: a disease gene on chromosomes one to twenty-two.

66
00:03:39.752 --> 00:03:43.069
X-linked: on the X, and more common in males.

67
00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:46.091
Locus: where a gene sits on a chromosome.

68
00:03:46.091 --> 00:03:49.997
Chromosomes: lengths of DNA carrying genes, in pairs.

69
00:03:49.997 --> 00:03:52.576
Genome: all the DNA in the nucleus.

70
00:03:52.576 --> 00:03:56.261
Autosome: a chromosome numbered one to twenty-two.

71
00:03:56.261 --> 00:03:58.546
Sex chromosome: the X or the Y.

72
00:03:58.546 --> 00:04:02.305
Variation: differences in genes across populations.

73
00:04:02.305 --> 00:04:06.211
Inheritance: how genes pass from parents to children.
